    {
      "text": "UAE Labour Law No. 8 of 1980 established the concept of \"arbitrary dismissal\" (no-fault dismissal) in UAE labour law. In essence, any dismissal of employees by an employer that did not fall within the list of specific grounds for dismissal stipulated by law constitutes an arbitrary dismissal. Article 123 of the foregoing law established the employer’s obligation, in the event of arbitrary dismissal, to pay the employee compensation in an amount not exceeding the employee’s salary for three months. \n\nUpon the adoption of new UAE Law No. 33 of 2021 \"On the Regulation of Labour Relations,\" the previous approach to the payment of compensation has been significantly modified. Article 47 of the new law stipulates that an employer is only required to pay compensation of up to three times the employee’s salary if the employee’s dismissal is due to the employee having initiated a dispute against the employer in court or with the Ministry of Labour and Emiratisation. \n\nIn all other cases, employers have the right to unilaterally terminate their employment relationships with employees without paying such compensation. In particular, this approach was confirmed in Decision No. 232/2022 of the Dubai Court of Cassation of 20 December 2022, and Decision No. 125/2022 of the Abu Dhabi Court of Cassation of 14 December 2022.",
