      ],
      "style": "Normal",
      "has_footnotes": false,
      "footnote_refs": [],
      "footnote_texts": {},
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "text": "To protect employees against unemployment, including as a result of the arbitrary termination of an employment contract by the employer, UAE Law No. 12 of 2022, \"On the Unemployment Insurance Programme,\" was enacted. Notably, insurance is mandatory, and the obligation to obtain insurance lies with employees themselves rather than with employers. Failure to comply with the insurance requirement entails a fine for the employee of AED 400 (Article 9(1) of Ministerial Decision No. 604 of 2022). \n\nIf an employment contract is terminated, an employee is entitled to receive a payment from the insurance company for a period of three months in the amount of 60% of his/her monthly salary (the average salary for the last six months of employment) (Article 6(1) of UAE Law No. 12 of 2022). If the individual finds new employment before the end of the three-month period, the insurance benefits are terminated. \n\nIn accordance with Cabinet of Ministers Resolution No. 97 of 2022, employees are divided into two categories: (i) employees with a monthly salary not exceeding AED 16,000 and (ii) employees with a monthly salary exceeding AED 16,000. The category to which an employee belongs affects both the amount of the insurance premium and the maximum amount of insurance benefits.. Therefore, individuals in the first category receive insurance benefits amounting to up to 60% of their monthly salary, but in any case, no more than AED 10,000 per month. For the second category of employees, the same rule applies, with a maximum limit of AED 20,000.",
